oai_dc:dc.dc:description:
This note describes the eviction of nesting White-throated Treecreepers Cormobates leucophaea from a nest box by a Sugar Glider Petaurus breviceps in central Victoria. Predation of the birds and eggs was not observed.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;

Contrary to conventional views, Thick-billed Grasswrens Amytornis modestus in the&nbsp;Northern Territory were not distributed continuously. They occurred as two subspecies&nbsp;separated by up to 200 km and in distinct habitats. The nominate A.m. modestus is now&nbsp;presumed long extinct, but formerly occurred in a limited area of central Australia in valley&nbsp;floors of the upper Finke River drainage, whereas a small population presumed to be of the&nbsp;subspecies A.m. indulkanna occurs near the Northern Territory&ndash;South Australia border.&nbsp;About 100 km south of that population are further records of the latter subspecies, which&nbsp;occupies a large area of the western Lake Eyre drainage basin in South Australia. Further&nbsp;field searches are needed to establish both the size and degree of isolation of the small&nbsp;remnant Northern Territory population.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;

The ability of Torresian Crows Corvus orru&nbsp; in the Northern Territory to consume Cane Toads Bufo marinus without being poisoned is evidently an acquired behaviour. However, it may have been acquired on many occasions and need not involve cultural transmission of knowledge. There is insufficient information to determine the process by which these behaviours have become established.
&nbsp;
